    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom grew out of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, created by the Serbian revolution of 1804–1817 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>, and ruled from 1817 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">Obrenović dynasty</a>. The principality, under Ottoman suzerainty, gained de facto independence when the last Ottoman garrisons left in 1867, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 recognised its formal independence and added the districts of Nišava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a>, Toplica and Vranje. In the newly gained areas an estimated 49,000–130,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> were expelled, settling mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, events that marked the beginning of the Serbian-Albanian conflict; from 1830 to 1876 up to 150,000 Albanians had already emigrated or been expelled from the principality.</p><p>In 1882 the ruler of the principality, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> I, was proclaimed king. The new kingdom at first kept a foreign policy friendly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, in whose political and economic sphere it lay until 1903. Its elite was divided between liberals, who looked to the West, and conservatives, who looked back to an idealised medieval Serbian empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was a predominantly agrarian society, most of its people living in extended families called zadruga, communities of property, work and authority in which fathers and grandfathers held absolute power. After independence the estates of the Muslim aristocracy were broken up, so that, unusually for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, the peasantry owned its land, though through the zadruga. The population rose by 71.3% between 1880 and 1914, one of the highest birthrates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and poverty was extreme: between 1910 and 1914 two thirds of farmers could not reach an existential minimum, and by January–February about 46% of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> suffered food insecurity.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> rose from 16.5 million francs in 1880 to 903.8 million in 1914, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> had only six millionaires besides the royal family in 1900. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> obliged Serbia to build the railway from Belgrade to Vranje; Serbian Railways were formed in 1881 and regular traffic between Belgrade and Niš began in 1884. The Morava Valley carried the route linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbo-bulgarian-war">Serbo-Bulgarian War</a> of 14–28 November 1885 ended in defeat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> won the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-slivnitsa">Battle of Slivnitsa</a> and advanced to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a>, and withdrew only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> declared it would join on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s side; the peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 19 February 1886 left the border unchanged. In 1912 Serbia allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and Montenegro. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a> and Novi Pazar, defeated the Ottoman army at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a> and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> vilayet. Lazër Mjeda, Archbishop of Skopje, estimated that 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> were killed by Serbian forces during and after the conflict.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a> of 1913 Serbia, Greece, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and Montenegro fought Bulgaria over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>. After Austria-Hungary declared war on 28 July 1914, a combined Austro-Hungarian, German and Bulgarian invasion occupied Serbia in 1915. The 135,000 soldiers of the Serbian Army retreated through Albania to Corfu and in 1916 joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Salonika front</a>. Serbia lost 28% of its pre-war population in the war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two dynasties ruled the kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> I reigned from 6 March 1882 until his abdication on 6 March 1889, after the lost war and the total electoral victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-radical-party">People's Radical Party</a>, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> Grujić and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> had come to power in 1888 with a new constitution based on the liberal Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. His son Alexander I, who succeeded him, assumed the throne in 1893 and dismissed the constitution in 1894. On the night of 28–29 May 1903 officers killed Alexander I and Queen Draga in the Royal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">House of Obrenović</a>.</p><p>The Skupština then invited Peter Karađorđević, who reigned as Peter I from 15 June 1903 to 1 December 1918. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> was created, with the military Black Hand society operating behind the scenes; the People's Radical Party governed after the 1906 elections. In 1890 the country was divided into 15 districts (okruzi) and counties (srezovi), Belgrade and Niš having special status; in August 1913 eleven new districts were formed in the conquered lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> gained citizen rights.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1903 the new dynasty relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and closer cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, signing a friendship treaty in 1904 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> in 1905; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> answered with the Pig war. Its annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> in October 1908, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> had hoped to expand, set off the Bosnian Crisis of 1908–1909, which permanently damaged relations between Austria-Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Serbia and was among the causes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a> of May 1913 recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> as Serbian, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 1913 gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar-macedonia">Vardar Macedonia</a>, though the new Principality of Albania kept it from the Adriatic.</p><p>On 28 June 1914 Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, an act behind which stood the secret Serbian officers' organisation Black Hand, which aimed to break the South Slav provinces away from Austria-Hungary. The assassination set off the chain of events that drew Russia and the major European powers into war. In culture, Serbian artists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a> showed in the Serbian pavilion at the International Exhibition of Art of 1911.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom ended not in defeat but in union. After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> at the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which had lost 28% of its pre-war population, went through radical changes within days of its liberation. In 1916 Montenegro had been conquered by Austria-Hungary. Serbia united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, and on 28 November 1918 it absorbed the Kingdom of Montenegro at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica-assembly">Podgorica Assembly</a>.</p><p>On 1 December 1918 Serbia united with the newly created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> to form the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, later known as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Peter I's reign over Serbia ended that day, the day the new kingdom was proclaimed. The new country continued under the Serbian monarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a>, and in August 1921 Prince Alexandar I became king. The territories Serbia had annexed in 1912–1913, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar-macedonia">Vardar Macedonia</a>, passed with it into the new state; Vardar Macedonia is today the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">Republic of North Macedonia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, proclaimed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> I in 1882, was a poor agrarian monarchy of zadruga households, ruled first by the Obrenović and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-coup-serbia">May Coup</a> of 1903 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a>. It nearly doubled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, was overrun in 1915 and in 1918 formed the core of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</em></p>

